SELECT 'eb4ab79c-12bb-7382-8adc-52630f4d4dd5', 'b2c2fec2-151a-7fd3-aa67-b923fa664283', 'Introduction to C++', 'Introduction to C++', 'A compiled, statically typed, multi-paradigm language that gives you C-level control of memory with high-level abstractions that cost nothing at run time.
C++ — a general-purpose language created by Bjarne Stroustrup (1979, “C with Classes”). Source is compiled ahead of time to native machine code — no virtual machine, no garbage collector.
Why C++?
Performance
Compiles to native code; you decide where every object lives (stack, heap, static). Used where latency matters: games, trading, browsers, databases.
Zero-overhead abstractions
“What you don''t use, you don''t pay for; what you do use, you couldn''t hand-code better.” Templates and inlining make std::sort as fast as hand-written C.
Multi-paradigm
Procedural (like C), object-oriented (classes, virtual functions), generic (templates) and functional (lambdas, ranges) — mix as needed.
Deterministic cleanup
Destructors run at a known point (end of scope) — the basis of RAII, C++''s answer to resource management.
C vs C++
Feature	C	C++
Paradigm	Procedural	Procedural + OOP + generic + functional
Structs	Data only	class/struct with methods, access control, inheritance
Memory	malloc / free	new / delete, but prefer containers & smart pointers
Strings	char* + <string.h>	std::string, std::string_view
Pass by	Value or pointer	Value, pointer or reference (int&)
Overloading	No	Function & operator overloading
Generic code	Macros / void*	Templates, concepts
Errors	Return codes, errno	Exceptions (plus return codes, std::optional)
Namespaces	No (one global scope)	namespace
Standard library	Small (stdio, stdlib…)	STL: containers, algorithms, threads, chrono, …
Compatibility
C++ is almost a superset of C — most C compiles as C++, but not all (e.g. int *p = malloc(4); needs a cast in C++, and class/new are keywords).
Your first program
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Hello, C++!\n";
    int year = 2020;
    std::cout << "Standard: C++" << year % 100 << ''\n'';
    return 0;   // optional in main: falling off the end returns 0
}
Hello, C++!
Standard: C++20
The standards
Standard	Headline features
C++98/03	STL, templates, exceptions, std::string
C++11	The “modern C++” reset: auto, lambdas, move semantics, nullptr, smart pointers, range-for, constexpr, threads
C++14	Generic lambdas, make_unique, relaxed constexpr
C++17	Structured bindings, optional/variant/any, string_view, if constexpr, filesystem
C++20	Concepts, ranges, coroutines, modules, <=>, std::format, std::span
C++23	std::print, std::expected, ranges::to, deducing this
Version
These notes use C++20 (every example is compiled with clang++ -std=c++20). C++23 features are mentioned where relevant.
Is C++ an object-oriented language?
It supports OOP but doesn''t force it. It''s multi-paradigm: you can write procedural code, generic code with templates, or functional-style code with lambdas and ranges. A C++ program doesn''t need a single class.
Why is C++ faster than Java or Python?
It compiles straight to native code with aggressive optimisation (inlining, templates specialised per type), has no garbage-collector pauses and no interpreter/JIT warm-up, and lets you control memory layout (contiguous std::vector, objects on the stack). The trade-off is that memory safety is your responsibility.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Introduction to C++'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Introduction to C++'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'A compiled, statically typed, multi-paradigm language that gives you C-level control of memory with high-level abstractions that cost nothing at run time.
