{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is an rvalue reference?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A reference declared with "},{"type":"text","text":"&&","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" that binds only to rvalues (temporaries or objects cast with "},{"type":"text","text":"std::move","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":"). It lets a function overload specifically for “this object is expiring”, so it can steal resources instead of copying. Inside the function, a named rvalue reference is itself an lvalue."}]}]}},"revision":1},{"id":"86b1b44f-082b-7812-a720-1891e6d56de6","type":"question","position":"aE","content":{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why should move constructors be noexcept?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Containers like "},{"type":"text","text":"std::vector","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" promise the strong exception guarantee when growing. They can only move elements into the new buffer if moving can''t throw (otherwise a half-moved vector couldn''t be restored), so they check "},{"type":"text","text":"std::is_nothrow_move_constructible","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and fall back to copying."}]}]}},"revision":1}]'::jsonb, '019a118c-f480-7000-8000-000000000001', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-10-07 03:23:53+00'
