{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What''s the difference between a pointer and a reference?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A pointer is an object holding an address: it can be null, re-pointed, and has its own address; you dereference it explicitly. A reference is an "},{"type":"text","text":"alias","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" for an existing object: it must be initialised, can''t be null or re-seated, and is used with ordinary syntax. Prefer references for parameters; use pointers when “no object” or re-pointing is needed."}]}]}},"revision":1},{"id":"7170739a-6fbd-7e96-819e-e30be114ada8","type":"question","position":"aM","content":{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the size of a pointer?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It depends on the platform, not the pointee: 8 bytes on 64-bit systems, 4 on 32-bit. "},{"type":"text","text":"sizeof(char*) == sizeof(double*)","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":". (Pointers to members can be larger.)"}]}]}},"revision":1}]'::jsonb, '019a118c-f480-7000-8000-000000000001', TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-10-07 03:23:53+00'
